  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front pushes through this morning as a low pressure\nsystem slides across Ontario, bringing scattered showers from\nwest to east across central NY. Cold air filtering in behind the\nfront will allow lake effect showers to develop across portions\nof Onondaga, Madison, and southern Oneida counties. These\nshowers should linger into the late morning or early afternoon\nbefore high pressure building in from the west brings an end to\nprecipitation. With cooler air in place, highs will only reach\nthe mid to upper 50s across central NY and the upper 50s to low\n60s in northeast PA. It will also be windy today, with gusts up\nto 30-35 mph. \n\nCooler temperatures will become more noticeable tonight as high\npressure builds in and skies clear, with lows falling into the\nmid 30s to low 40s across much of the area. Portions of northern\nOneida county could fall below freezing, with patchy frost\npossible elsewhere, especially where winds become calm. Model\nguidance continues to bring the center of the high overhead\nlater Tuesday morning, which could keep winds elevated a little\nlonger and limit frost formation tonight. If the high shifts\neastwards more quickly, frost could become more widespread, with\nsome sheltered valleys potentially reaching freezing. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nHigh pressure settles over the region Tuesday, brining mostly\nsunny skies and a chilly start to the day. Northwest flow will \nkeep temperatures on the cooler side, with highs generally in \nthe 50s. Tuesday night could turn cold again, though \ntemperatures will depend on how quickly the ridge axis and \nsurface high shifts east. Lows could range from the low to mid \n30s to the low 40s, with clear skies and relatively calm winds \nwhich could favor cooler conditions. Warmer air returns towards\nthe end of the week, with highs climbing into the 60s and \novernight lows generally in the 40s. A weak passing wave may \nbring scattered rain showers to northern portions of the CWA \nThursday, though most locations should remain dry through the \nend of the week.",
